      "text": "Adding 6 to both sides and taking one square root gives\n\\[\\sqrt{\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} - 6 + 6}^2 = x + y + 2\\sqrt{-6x^2/y},\\]so\n\\[\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} + 6 \\le \\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} + \\sqrt{\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} - 6 + 6}^2 \\le \\sqrt{\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} - 6 + 6}^2 + \\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\\]Then (subtracting twice the RHS and dividing by $\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$),\n\\[\\frac{2\\sqrt{(x + y)^2}}{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} + \\sqrt{-6x^2/y}}^2 \\le \\sqrt{\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} - 6}\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{(x + y)^2}}{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} - \\sqrt{-6x^2/y}}^2.\\]Squaring the expression in the middle,\n\\[\\frac{4(x^2 + y^2)}{(x + y)^2 + 2\\sqrt{-6x^2/y}\\sqrt{3(x^2 + y^2)}} \\le 20.\\]Since $\\frac{2(x^2 + y^2)}{x + y + \\sqrt{-6x^2/y}} \\ge 2\\sqrt{\\frac{(x^2 + y^2)}{-6x^2/y}}$ and $\\frac{4(x^2 + y^2)}{x y (x + y)^2 + 2\\sqrt{-6x^2/y}\\sqrt{3(x^2 + y^2)}} \\le 20,$ equality holds when $x + y = \\sqrt{6}xy.$  Then\n\\[(\\sqrt{6} - 1) = \\frac{x + y}{xy} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6}xy}{xy} = \\sqrt{6},\\]which implies $x + y = \\sqrt{6}xy.$  Then $x^2 + y^2 = y^2 + (\\sqrt{6} - x)^2 = 2y^2 - 2x\\sqrt{6} + 6.$  Thus,\n\\[\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} + 6 = \\frac{2y^2 - 2x\\sqrt{6} + 12}{2y^2 - 2x\\sqrt{6} + 6} = 3 \\tag*{} \\blacksquare\\].\nSource: Can it be generalized?\n\nLet's solve the given problem and see if we can generalize it.\n\nThe original problem is to find the maximum value of \\(\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\).\n\nFirst, let's add and subtract \\(6\\) inside the square root to find the maximum value:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} - 6 + 6}^2 = x + y + 2\\sqrt{-6x^2/y}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} + 6 \\le \\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} + \\sqrt{\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} - 6 + 6}^2 \\le \\sqrt{\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} - 6 + 6}^2 + \\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\n\\]\n\nThis form indicates that we can find the maximum value by finding the critical points of the 